d transform our  understanding of planet earth you see  studying the moon is not just an  abstract thought we're really trying to  understand  ourselves where we come from how did  earth form  what can we learn about our planet  and the origin of life and the moon  may hold the key because  one of the things we're going to find  when we go back to the moon  one of the things we're going to find  when we have stations on the moon and we  have geologists exploring  roving the surface collecting more and  more rocks is we're going to find  fragments of the most ancient  earth earth meteorites  that landed on the moon think think  about that you have a giant impact  on the early earth it smashes the crust  it smashes the surface  fragments of earth are blown out into  space  and some of those are going to fall on  our moon  they're going to litter the surface and  when we find  those rocks they'll be more than 4  billion  years old they'll be the oldest rocks we  have ever seen from earth it will tell  us about our earliest history  how the oceans formed how the atmosphere  formed whether there was  life in those earliest times  and you say oh that just sounds like  science fiction that can't possibly be  true  but it turns out there was a recent  finding of a  meteorite from the moon  what's called a breccia a brechus lots  of tiny tiny fragments  of rocks from all over the surface of  the moon that came together and got  cemented together  and one of those fragments  looks like a piece of earth  so there's already this very tantalizing  hint that we have a piece  of ancient earth preserved in a  meteorite that came from from the moon  and if we go back to the moon we're  certain to find  bigger chunks chunks of earth i just  can't wait for that to happen  that's going to be a major finding it  will transform our understanding of the  history  of our planet in 1969  at that time of the landing i was an  undergraduate at mit  and it turns out my thesis advisor  because 