oon dust that  kind of rises and spreads around i mean  we're breathing the stuff  and and it's all over his fingers he's  got moon dust on his fingers and he  eventually transfers them into three  vials but on that glass  scene the sheet of paper that he used  they were actually day ones fingerprints  in moon dust  after the guards left we kind of took  the piece of paper and we had it framed  and mounted  there was no label on it you know you  weren't supposed to keep  samples of nasa owns them all but we we  kept them anyway  we kept a frame with dave once  fingerprints and moon dust that was my  my first recollection and i wasn't  studying the moon then  but a few years later i had the  opportunity to come to the carnegie  institution and my first job as a  scientist was to  extract tiny grains from these lunar  fines these very fine  sand-like grains the colorful ones the  reds and the greens and the  and the yellows and so forth and by the  way the way you pick up a sand grain  it's funny you have a needle  and in order to make a sticky you  basically brush the needle against your  nose because there's a little bit of oil  and then that  you can stick you can stick the needle  down and pick up an individual grain  and then transfer it to a glass slide  and then very carefully  document it measure its composition  measure its crystal structure measure  its optical properties  i did that for dozens and dozens of  grains and every time i look down the  microscope every time i move to grain  i was thinking my gosh this is a real  piece of the moon this was brought back  by  our astronauts this this is something  that no one in history has ever been  able to do before and now we've got this  treasure trove and we're learning from  it it was for  a young scientist it was it was amazing  opportunity  the apollo missions their importance  just  it can't be overstated because  they were a source of scientific  fascination of course but there also was  the political ide